{
  "teamId": 144,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Atlanta Braves wrote one of the most improbable championship stories in recent baseball memory. They stumbled out of the gate in April, finishing 12-14 in the opening month, and the early weeks were defined as much by relief pitcher misfires as by the surprising heroics of journeyman Pablo Sandoval, who clubbed multiple clutch home runs to keep the Braves competitive. The team hovered around mediocrity through May and June, battling a bullpen that leaked runs at the worst moments — Will Smith and A.J. Minter combined for several gut-punch losses — and the team sat well below .500 as summer arrived.\n\nThe turning point came in late July and exploded through August, when the Braves caught fire. After losing Ronald Acuña Jr. to a season-ending ACL tear in early July, Atlanta responded not with surrender but with transformation. The front office acquired Jorge Soler, Joc Pederson, and Eddie Rosario in a flurry of deadline moves, and the roster remade itself into something tougher and more dangerous. An 18-8 August — the best month on the calendar — announced to the rest of the NL that Atlanta was no longer playing for moral victories.\n\nSeptember brought a grinding push to the division title, with Soler, Pederson, Travis d'Arnaud, and Ozzie Albies delivering critical hits as the Braves locked up the NL East. Then October belonged to Eddie Rosario, who put on one of the most stunning NLCS performances in postseason history, and to a Braves team that took down the defending champion Los Angeles Dodgers in six games before stunning the Houston Astros in the World Series.\n\nFrom 12-14 in April to World Series champions — the 2021 Braves proved that a season is never a straight line, and that the best stories are the ones nobody saw coming.",
